00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:07.490
Kerry: As a teacher,
you're not just a teacher.

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:12.680
And what I think one of the strengths
of our school is that we have

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:16.309
such strong relationships with the
families of these young people.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:21.849
And I think particularly during COVID
and these lockdown scenarios  we've

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:27.350
had to be more supportive of the home
environment as well as at school.

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:37.050
So, I know families who have struggled
with sharing time between the young

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:41.940
person with autism and their siblings
and feeling like most of their time is

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:43.660
being drawn towards that young person.

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:49.920
And other siblings may be sort of by
the wayside and feeling guilty for that.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:56.880
I've supported families who, during
first lockdown, saw a big increase

00:00:56.910 --> 00:01:01.530
in challenging behaviors because they
couldn't go for community access.

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:04.349
You know, there was no swimming,
there was no cinema, there was

00:01:04.349 --> 00:01:07.260
no trips on the trams, which were
all part of their normal routine.

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:09.780
And that was a big struggle for them.

00:01:11.145 --> 00:01:16.770
I've also supported a family who
the young person with autism was

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:20.850
the only sibling until she was 13.

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:22.420
And then mum was having a baby.

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:29.580
So there was, mum was worried about how
she might cope with that because she

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:33.900
quite liked to be in control of every
situation and things had to be just

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:37.259
so, and, you know, quiet when you were
told quiet and things have to be in a

00:01:37.259 --> 00:01:43.515
certain place, and, you know, so we put
a lot of work in to why babies cry, why

00:01:43.525 --> 00:01:49.095
they won't just shush, you know, how she
might be able to help mum, what she can

00:01:49.095 --> 00:01:52.455
do when she's finding it a bit too much.

00:01:52.515 --> 00:01:56.835
And that was, there was bumpy moments
and she wasn't too keen on baby brother

00:01:56.835 --> 00:02:00.975
when he arrived, because there wasn't
much interaction there for her, but

00:02:00.975 --> 00:02:03.735
now he's crawling and he's starting to
walk, you know, they've got a really

00:02:03.735 --> 00:02:09.285
strong relationship that was able to
happen because we have that strong

00:02:09.555 --> 00:02:13.275
relationship with mum and home, and
we've supported them as much as possible.

00:02:14.275 --> 00:02:18.515
Zoe T: I've experienced the devastating
impact that autism can have on

00:02:18.515 --> 00:02:20.535
families myself on my own family.

00:02:21.485 --> 00:02:25.625
That's not to say that the, you
know, I'm not demonising the

00:02:25.625 --> 00:02:32.115
autism or the child it's just, that
is, you know, it's a fact that.

00:02:34.155 --> 00:02:39.675
I believe parents aren't supported to
properly understand the child's autism

00:02:39.765 --> 00:02:42.255
and to properly manage the child's autism.

00:02:42.705 --> 00:02:47.055
But it's going to have a negative impact
on many families where the child is

00:02:47.175 --> 00:02:52.455
presenting with sort of challenging
behavior that comes as a result of

00:02:53.205 --> 00:03:00.030
parents using, usually, using an
imperative communication style whether

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:03.420
putting lots of pressure on the child,
making lots of demands because they're

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:06.420
not understanding that actually that's
